WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:04.779
After months of waiting and endless speculation,
the trailer for the fourth Avengers movie

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:05.960
has finally arrived.

00:00:06.209 --> 00:00:10.260
For one thing, the the trailer reveals the
title for the feature as Avengers: Endgame,

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:13.820
confirming previously leaked info indicating
that that might be the case.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.020
But beyond that, there's a world of mystery
to this footage, showing us our first official

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:21.820
glimpse of the Marvel Cinematic Universe post-Thanos'
decimation.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:25.380
You've already watched and rewatched this
thing about a thousand times, so let's break

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.900
down some of the small details you might have
missed in the trailer for Avengers: Endgame.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.560
Thanos - now retired to his quiet life as
a big purple space farmer - has put up his

00:00:34.569 --> 00:00:37.559
armor for good as an incredibly imposing scarecrow.

00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:41.609
You can actually see the scarecrow in Avengers:
Infinity War, in the lower right corner of

00:00:41.609 --> 00:00:45.889
the frame during the movie's ending sequence
of Thanos looking over his crops toward the

00:00:45.889 --> 00:00:47.920
shining sun on some unknown world.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.469
But this image has some extra significance
you might not be aware of.

00:00:51.469 --> 00:00:55.579
In the six-issue Infinity Gauntlet comic book
series - which served as the inspiration for

00:00:55.579 --> 00:01:00.159
the two-part Infinity War story - the scarecrow
is a striking image from the final issue.

00:01:00.159 --> 00:01:03.230
In the comic, the scarecrow appears to be
Thanos' only company.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:07.280
When the heroes come upon it, they regard
it as "an empty shell of what was", which

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:11.579
may imply some interesting twists in store
for the final showdown between the Avengers

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:12.360
and Thanos.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.880
As for Thanos himself, he's only seen briefly
in the trailer, running his hand over some

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.530
plantlife with his burnt-up Infinity Gauntlet.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.620
Does he have the strength for a second round?

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.000
We'll see — after all:

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.880
"We're in the endgame now."

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.920
After sitting out the events of Infinity War,
Jeremy Renner's Clint Barton has returned

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:36.850
for the fourth Avengers adventure - and something
is very different about him.

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:41.420
He's got a new haircut, a new samurai-inspired
outfit, and he seems to be stomping the streets

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:45.549
of Japan, hunting down some sharp-dressed
victims and leaving them dead in the street

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:46.560
for some unknown end.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:47.640
You alright, Clint?

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.640
This is more than just a costume change - it's
a whole new identity, one that Natasha seems

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:53.869
somewhat shocked to see.

00:01:53.869 --> 00:01:58.200
Rumors have been swirling about the meaning
of Clint's absence from Infinity War since

00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.880
before the movie even came out.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:04.110
The prevailing theory has been that Barton
sheds his old identity - and maybe even a

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:08.560
little bit of his morality - when his entire
family presumably gets dusted at the end of

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:09.440
Infinity War.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.470
We didn't see it go down on screen, so we
don't know for sure if that that's what happened

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:14.240
- but c'mon.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:18.860
Do you think Clint is booking flights to Japan
to go stab people if he's got a wife and kids

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:19.360
at home?

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:24.060
In a bittersweet twist, it looks as though
Tony Stark has commandeered the newest vessel

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:28.241
of the Guardians of the Galaxy, the ​Benatar​,
which took the place of the ​Milano​ after

00:02:28.241 --> 00:02:31.140
its destruction in ​Guardians of the Galaxy
Vol. 2​.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:33.660
Of course, not all is as bleak as it first
appears.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.750
For one, Tony does not seem to be alone on
this sad, doomed trip across the universe.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:42.120
It looks like Nebula is there with him, hitching
a ride after her ship was destroyed during

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.960
her showdown with Thanos on Titan.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:47.440
Her blue fingers can be seen on someone's
shoulder - probably Tony's.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.210
Considering she's more robot than mortal being
at this point thanks to Thanos' tinkering,

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:55.820
it's possible that the lack of oxygen and
food on the ship isn't as big a deal to her

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:56.820
as it is to Tony.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:01.860
For months and months, rumors have swirled
about the Avengers going back in time to undo

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:06.900
the destruction Thanos has caused, a theory
supported by leaks indicating that the production

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.960
was rebuilding sets from older movies.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:13.400
It's hard to think of any other reason why
Cap would be looking at a photo of Peggy with

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.820
such significance, unless he was about to
go and find her for some reason.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:20.840
Other set leaks have indicated​ that the
movie may have built a new set for Camp Lehigh,

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:25.480
the boot camp in Captain America: The First
Avenger where a pre-super serum Steve Rogers

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:26.720
first proved his worth.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.530
The base, located in New Jersey, was also
visited in the modern era in ​Captain America:

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:34.720
The Winter Soldier​ - and more importantly,
it was destroyed​.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:39.720
So if the movie is taking us to Camp Lehigh
as the leaks suggest, then it's probably not

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:40.800
going to be in the present day.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.860
And that would help explain Cap going back
to his outfit from the Winter Soldier - sort

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:48.540
of like when Star Trek characters have to
go back in time and try their best to blend

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.940
in so as not to screw up the timestream:

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:54.960
"Uh-oh."

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:57.800
"Wow!"

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:02.200
It's a blink-and-you'll-miss-it moment, but
​​the Avengers: Endgame ​trailer revealed

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:06.340
that Shuri, the woman who we thought might
be Wakanda's last hope, is among the missing.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:10.460
During the sequence of Bruce Banner looking
at the Avengers' list of missing people, the

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:14.560
image of Letitia Wright's Shuri can briefly
be seen among the presumed dead.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:18.660
With this, we're meant to believe that she
dies offscreen, snapped into dust just like

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:19.320
her brother.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.840
While the team's intelligence is wrong about
Ant-Man, who is indeed missing but not dead,

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.660
we have no reason to believe that Shuri made
it out alive.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.300
But while we're on the subject of Ant-Man...

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.160
Clint Barton isn't the only Avenger making
a triumphant return.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:35.960
Now that most of the heavy-hitters are dead
and dusted, it's time to welcome Scott Lang

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:36.720
to the party.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:39.120
"Welcome to the party, pal!"

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:44.560
While Natasha and Steve are moping around
Avengers HQ, they get a video message from

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.700
Ant-Man, who seems to be at the gates of the
facility.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:48.020
"Can you buzz me in?"

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:52.620
That's exciting enough - but what's more exciting
is the gear that Scott brought with him.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:55.400
"sorry"

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:56.820
"My bad."

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:02.060
Last time we saw Ant-Man's Ant-Van, it had
been turned into a makeshift laboratory, kitted

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:04.010
out with a portal to the Quantum Realm.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:08.060
In the post-credits sequence of Ant-Man and
the Wasp, Scott entered the Quantum Realm

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:12.020
and appeared to be stuck there, with his partners
being dusted before they could pull him back.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.560
Scott sure looked doomed - so what gives?

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:18.660
Right before Scott jumped into the Quantum
Realm, he got this warning from the original

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:19.160
Wasp:

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.620
"And don't get sucked into a time vortex.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:23.920
We won't be able to save you."

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:29.140
It seems pretty possible that Scott used a
vortex to exit the Realm, depositing himself

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:33.000
in the future - and now he's brought the time
travel tech to Avengers HQ.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:37.240
Who would have thought Ant-Man would be so
important when it came time to save the world?

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:37.979
Probably this guy did:

